<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shenyang</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mukden, Shengjing</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shenyang, known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> as Mukden, is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaoning">Liaoning</a> province in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nurhaci">Nurhaci</a> made it the capital of the Later Jin in 1625, and it remained the Manchu capital until the move to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> in 1644; its Mukden Palace survives. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-japanese-war">Russo-Japanese War</a>'s Battle of Mukden was fought nearby in 1905, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mukden-incident">Mukden Incident</a> of 1931 began the Japanese occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shenyang.jpg" alt="Shenyang" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: E2568 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%B2%88%E9%98%B3%E9%9D%92%E5%B9%B4%E5%85%AC%E5%9B%AD.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Shenyang lies in the central part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaoning">Liaoning</a> province. Its western districts are on the alluvial plain of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-river">Liao River</a> system and its eastern part reaches into the forested hinterland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-paektu">Changbai Mountains</a>. The name means the yang side of the Shen River, the old name of the Hun River, which runs on the southern side of the city; in Chinese naming custom a river's north bank is the sunny, or yang, side. Humans lived here as early as 8,000 years ago, and the remains of the Xinle culture, a late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> society over 6,800 years old, are kept in a museum in Huanggu District.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the 14th century the city came under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a>, for which it was an important military stronghold. In 1625 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nurhaci">Nurhaci</a> captured Shenyang and moved his whole administration to it. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> name was Simiyan hoton, and in 1634 the official name became Mukden, or Shengjing, meaning rising capital, from the Manchu word mukdembi, to rise. The imperial palace was built from 1626 on Nurhaci's orders, with more than 300 rooms and 20 gardens.</p><p>After the First Sino-Japanese War Japan obtained the Liaodong Peninsula by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-shimonoseki">Treaty of Shimonoseki</a> in 1895 but had to give it up under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/triple-intervention">Triple Intervention</a>. A secret treaty of 1896 allowed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> to build a railway through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boxer-rebellion">Boxer Rebellion</a> of 1900 Russian forces occupied most of Manchuria, making Mukden a Russian stronghold. The Battle of Mukden of 1905 was fought south of the city, and Japanese influence grew afterwards. In 1914 the city took back the name Shenyang, though Mukden stayed in use in English and in Japan for decades.</p><p>The Mukden incident of 1931 led to the Japanese invasion and occupation of the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> and the puppet state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchukuo">Manchukuo</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> occupied the city in August 1945, and British and American reports say Soviet troops looted and terrorised its people. The Communists captured it in 1948 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaoshen-campaign">Liaoshen campaign</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Shenyang is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaoning">Liaoning</a> province, with 9,070,093 residents at the 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. It has been a centre of heavy industry since the 1930s, and many factories were built in Tiexi district in the first five-year plan, 1951 to 1956. After the 1980s heavy industry declined and hundreds of thousands of workers were laid off. It is the railway hub of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shenyang” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the <b>Mukden</b> incident, a bombing of a Japanese-controlled railway at Liutiaogou that Japanese officers had staged, Japan invaded and occupied Manchuria in 1931.</blockquote><a href="/empire/manchukuo">Manchukuo · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Nurhaci declared war on the Ming Empire in 1618, won a great victory at Sarhu in 1619, took Liaoyang and <b>Shenyang</b> and in 1625 made Shenyang (Mukden) his capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/later-jin-1616-1636">Later Jin (1616-1636)</a></li></ul></section>
